#!/bin/sh
# script/board-console: read a real board's serial console, log every byte, and stop on a
# deadline (milestone 216).
#
#   script/board-console                      # watch until the kernel banner, 120s cap
#   script/board-console --for 30m --until none   # sustained watching, for a stress run
#   script/board-console --replay target/board-console-1756...log   # re-read a capture
#   script/board-console --tally target/radon-soak-1757...log        # what the lottery drew (249)
#   script/board-console --exposure target/argon-soak-1759...log --machine argon --build <sha> --start '<utc>'
#                                             # the curve's exposure row for each soaked boot (225)
#   script/board-console --stop               # end a rebooting soak at its next draw (249, 324)
#   script/board-console --stop-after 50      # end one with exactly fifty samples in it
#   script/board-console --board xenon        # a board whose firmware prologue is not radon's
#   script/board-console --until sweep-done   # wait for a job-mix sweep to finish (324)
#
# `script/console` is QEMU. This is the other one: an actual serial port on an actual board, at
# 115200 8N1, with the log written to a file whatever happens. It recognises the boot sequence
# notes/visionfive2.md's bench runbook records (SPL, OpenSBI, U-Boot, `Starting kernel ...`, ours)
# and returns a different exit status for each way a session can end, so a bench script can tell
# them apart:
#
# **What it recognises is two halves, and only one of them is a board's** (milestone 324 part 3,
# calef's ruling of 2026-09-19: *one tool with a board profile, and the profile is the firmware
# prologue only*). `--board <name>` chooses the firmware chain to expect. `radon` is the default
# and is the VisionFive 2's SPL, OpenSBI, U-Boot and handoff; `xenon` has an empty prologue,
# because it boots through PVH straight into our banner and its 2026-09-17 capture shows nothing
# before it. Everything from the kernel banner up is shared by every board and always was.
# `--until spl` against a board with no SPL is refused rather than waited out. argon has no profile
# on purpose: it has never booted nife, and a prologue read out of vendor documentation would be a
# guess wearing a measurement's clothes.
#
#   0  reached the stage asked for (or watched the whole duration with nothing announced)
#   1  the board announced a failure: a bad image header, a measured-boot refusal, a kernel panic
#   2  it spoke and then went quiet, which is what a hang looks like from this end
#   3  the time ran out with the stage unreached
#   4  the port could not be opened, or the arguments were wrong
#
# **It writes only what a named mode sends, and every byte it sends is printed into the log.** That
# is the invariant, ruled by calef on 2026-09-19 (milestone 324 part 4), replacing "it reads and
# never writes to the board". The second clause is not decoration: the log is the artifact a bench
# run is judged from, so a byte the board received that the capture does not show would make the
# capture a lie about the run.
#
# Today there is exactly one named mode and it sends exactly one byte. `--stop` ends milestone
# 249's self-rebooting soak, whose escape is any byte on this UART, and `--stop-after <n>` ends
# such a series with exactly the sample it was asked for. The byte goes out only after the board
# has announced an armed reboot loop, which is what proves there is a loop to stop, that the
# kernel's own receive drain is already behind us, and that the board is long past U-Boot's
# autoboot countdown. That countdown is the hazard the narrowness is about: milestone 249's lane
# sent this escape by detaching the console, hit the countdown with it, and paid a power cycle.
# This is not a keyboard, and it is narrow on purpose rather than for now.
#
# It still does not touch power. `--tally` still touches no port at all: it reads a capture that
# has already finished and reports what the thread-placement lottery drew on each boot in it
# (milestone 249). A tally has two exit statuses, 0 and 4, because there is no board left to have
# gone quiet. A stop mode adds no status either: an escape that was sent and never acknowledged is
# 3, the time running out with the requested thing unreached.
#
# See notes/board-console.md for the runbook and for what the recognition can and cannot tell
# apart, and notes/soak.md for the rebooting soak the tally is written for.
#
# Name: provisional, this lane's coinage (2026-09-01, milestone 216). Hyphens because script/
# entry points are hyphenated (AGENTS.md's naming table), and the `board-` prefix to sit beside
# `board-image`, the other entry point here that is about a real board; the pair now reads as one
# family, build the payload and then watch it boot. `console` alone was already taken by the QEMU
# one and could not be reused. `serial` was refused as a word for the wire rather than for the
# thing. Expect the integrator to surface it.

set -e
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

# This project's own QEMU, if `script/ci-qemu` has built one (helpers/qemu-path.sh says why
# PATH is the mechanism). A no-op on macOS and on any machine without that prefix.
. helpers/qemu-path.sh

exec cargo xtask board-console "$@"
